1 ACCOUNTING
 Displays login accounting information for the current user.

 Format: ACCOUNTING [/qualifier]

 Displays the login history, session durations, and resource usage
 for the current process or specified user account.

2 Parameters
 None.

2 Qualifiers
3 /FULL
 Displays complete accounting detail including CPU time,
 page faults, and I/O counts.

3 /SINCE
 Limits display to records since a specified date.

 Format: /SINCE=date

3 /USER
 Displays accounting for a specified username.

 Format: /USER=username

2 Examples

 $ ACCOUNTING
 $ ACCOUNTING /FULL

1 ANALYZE
 Analyzes system components and data structures.

 Format: ANALYZE component

 Used to examine system internals, disk structures, and
 error logs. Not all analysis modes are available in OVMX.

2 Parameters
3 component
 The system component to analyze. Valid values include
 DISK_STRUCTURE, ERROR_LOG, and SYSTEM.

2 Examples

 $ ANALYZE/DISK_STRUCTURE DKA0:
 $ ANALYZE/ERROR_LOG

1 APPEND
 Appends the contents of one file to the end of another file.

 Format: APPEND source-file destination-file

 The source file is copied and appended to the end of the
 destination file. If the destination does not exist, it is
 created.

2 Parameters
3 source-file
 The file whose contents will be appended to the destination.
 Wildcards are permitted.

3 destination-file
 The file to which the source contents are appended.

2 Qualifiers
3 /CONFIRM
 Prompts for confirmation before appending.

3 /LOG
 Displays each file as it is appended.

3 /NEW_VERSION
 Creates a new version of the destination file if one
 already exists.

2 Examples

 $ APPEND DATA.TMP RESULTS.DAT
 $ APPEND *.TXT COMBINED.TXT

1 ASSIGN
 Creates a logical name that translates to a specified equivalence
 string.

 Format: ASSIGN equivalence-name logical-name

 ASSIGN creates a logical name in the process logical name table.
 The equivalence name is the value to which the logical name
 translates. See also DEFINE, which uses the opposite argument
 order.

2 Parameters
3 equivalence-name
 The string to which the logical name translates. This is
 typically a device name, directory specification, or another
 logical name.

3 logical-name
 The logical name to be created.

2 Qualifiers
3 /TABLE
 Specifies the logical name table in which to create the
 logical name.

 Format: /TABLE=table-name

 Default is LNM$PROCESS_TABLE.

2 Examples

 $ ASSIGN SYS$SYSDEVICE:[MYDIR] WORK
 $ ASSIGN "node::disk:[dir]" REMOTE_DIR

1 ATTACH
 Transfers control to another process in your job tree.

 Format: ATTACH [process-name]

 Allows you to switch between interactive processes. The
 process you attach to must be part of your current job.

2 Parameters
3 process-name
 The name of the process to attach to. If omitted, attaches
 to the parent process.

2 Examples

 $ ATTACH SMITH_1

1 BACKUP
 Creates, restores, or lists VMS saveset files.

 Format: BACKUP source destination

 BACKUP creates portable archive files in VMS saveset format.
 Savesets contain file data, attributes, and directory structure.
 BACKUP can also restore from savesets and list their contents.

2 Parameters
3 source
 The source specification. For a save operation, this is a
 file specification (wildcards allowed). For a restore or
 list operation, this is a saveset file.

3 destination
 The destination specification. For a save operation, this
 is the saveset filename (conventionally ending in .BCK).
 For a restore operation, this is the target directory.

2 Qualifiers
3 /LIST
 Lists the contents of a saveset without restoring.

 Format: BACKUP/LIST saveset.BCK

3 /LOG
 Displays each file as it is processed.

3 /VERIFY
 Verifies the saveset after creation by reading it back
 and comparing checksums.

3 /CONFIRM
 Prompts before processing each file.

2 Examples

 $ BACKUP [MYDIR...]*.*;* MYBACKUP.BCK/SAVE
 $ BACKUP MYBACKUP.BCK/SAVE [RESTORE...]
 $ BACKUP/LIST MYBACKUP.BCK

1 CLOSE
 Closes a file that was previously opened with the OPEN command.

 Format: CLOSE logical-name

 Closes the file associated with the specified logical name and
 removes the logical name from the process table.

2 Parameters
3 logical-name
 The logical name assigned to the file when it was opened.

2 Examples

 $ CLOSE INFILE
 $ CLOSE OUTFILE

1 CONTINUE
 Resumes execution of a program that was interrupted.

 Format: CONTINUE

 CONTINUE resumes a program that was interrupted by Ctrl-Y.
 The program continues from the point at which it was
 interrupted.

2 Examples

 $ CONTINUE

1 CONVERT
 Converts files from one format to another.

 Format: CONVERT input-file output-file

 Converts record formats, file organizations, or file
 structures between different RMS file types.

2 Parameters
3 input-file
 The file to be converted.

3 output-file
 The name of the converted output file.

2 Qualifiers
3 /FDL
 Uses an FDL (File Definition Language) file to specify
 the output file characteristics.

 Format: /FDL=fdl-file

2 Examples

 $ CONVERT OLD.DAT NEW.DAT

1 COPY
 Copies one or more files to a new location.

 Format: COPY source-file destination-file

 Creates a copy of the source file with the specified
 destination name. Wildcards are permitted in the source
 specification.

2 Parameters
3 source-file
 The file to be copied. Wildcards are permitted.

3 destination-file
 The destination file specification.

2 Qualifiers
3 /CONFIRM
 Prompts before each copy operation.

3 /LOG
 Displays each file as it is copied.

3 /REPLACE
 Replaces existing files at the destination.

2 Examples

 $ COPY SOURCE.TXT DEST.TXT
 $ COPY *.DAT [.BACKUP]
 $ COPY/LOG FILE.TXT [OTHERDIR]FILE.TXT

1 CREATE
 Creates a new file or directory.

 Format: CREATE filespec
         CREATE/DIRECTORY directory-spec

 Without qualifiers, CREATE creates an empty file. With the
 /DIRECTORY qualifier, creates a new directory.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The name of the file or directory to create.

2 Qualifiers
3 /DIRECTORY
 Creates a directory rather than a file.

 Format: CREATE/DIRECTORY [.NEWDIR]

3 /LOG
 Displays the name of each file or directory created.

3 /OWNER_UIC
 Sets the owner UIC of the created file or directory.

 Format: /OWNER_UIC=uic

3 /PROTECTION
 Sets the protection of the created file or directory.

 Format: /PROTECTION=(S:RWED,O:RWED,G:RE,W:E)

2 Examples

 $ CREATE NEWFILE.TXT
 $ CREATE/DIRECTORY [.SUBDIR]
 $ CREATE/DIRECTORY/LOG [.PROJECT]

1 DEASSIGN
 Removes a logical name definition.

 Format: DEASSIGN logical-name

 Removes the specified logical name from the process logical
 name table. See also ASSIGN and DEFINE.

2 Parameters
3 logical-name
 The logical name to be removed.

2 Qualifiers
3 /TABLE
 Specifies the logical name table from which to remove
 the name.

 Format: /TABLE=table-name

2 Examples

 $ DEASSIGN WORK
 $ DEASSIGN/TABLE=LNM$SYSTEM SHARED_DIR

1 DEFINE
 Creates a logical name definition.

 Format: DEFINE logical-name equivalence-name

 DEFINE creates a logical name that translates to the specified
 equivalence string. Note that DEFINE uses the opposite argument
 order from ASSIGN.

2 Parameters
3 logical-name
 The logical name to be created.

3 equivalence-name
 The string to which the logical name translates.

2 Qualifiers
3 /TABLE
 Specifies the logical name table in which to create the
 definition.

 Format: /TABLE=table-name

3 /LOG
 Displays the logical name definition after creation.

2 Examples

 $ DEFINE MYDIR SYS$SYSDEVICE:[USERS.SMITH]
 $ DEFINE/TABLE=LNM$SYSTEM SHARED SYS$SYSDEVICE:[SHARED]

1 DELETE
 Deletes one or more files, or deletes a symbol definition.

 Format: DELETE filespec[,...]
         DELETE/SYMBOL symbol-name

 Removes the specified files from the disk. With the /SYMBOL
 qualifier, removes a DCL symbol definition.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The file or files to delete. Wildcards are permitted.
 Multiple files can be specified separated by commas.

2 Qualifiers
3 /CONFIRM
 Prompts before each delete operation.

3 /LOG
 Displays the name of each file as it is deleted.

3 /SYMBOL
 Deletes a DCL symbol instead of a file.

 Format: DELETE/SYMBOL symbol-name

3 /SYMBOL/GLOBAL
 Deletes a global DCL symbol.

 Format: DELETE/SYMBOL/GLOBAL symbol-name

2 Examples

 $ DELETE OLDFILE.TXT;*
 $ DELETE/CONFIRM *.TMP;*
 $ DELETE/SYMBOL MYVAR
 $ DELETE/LOG SCRATCH.*;*

1 DIFFERENCES
 Compares two files and displays the differences between them.

 Format: DIFFERENCES file-1 file-2

 Reads both files and produces a listing of lines that differ.
 Similar in concept to the Unix diff utility, but follows VMS
 conventions for output format.

2 Parameters
3 file-1
 The first file to compare.

3 file-2
 The second file to compare.

2 Qualifiers
3 /OUTPUT
 Directs the differences output to a file.

 Format: /OUTPUT=filespec

3 /IGNORE
 Specifies what differences to ignore.

 Format: /IGNORE=(SPACING, CASE, TRAILING_SPACES)

3 /PARALLEL
 Displays differences in a side-by-side format.

2 Examples

 $ DIFFERENCES FILE1.TXT FILE2.TXT
 $ DIFFERENCES/OUTPUT=DIFF.LIS PROG.C;2 PROG.C;1

1 DIRECTORY
 Lists the files in a directory.

 Format: DIRECTORY [filespec]

 Displays information about files in the specified or default
 directory. Without arguments, lists all files in the current
 default directory.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The files to list. Wildcards are permitted. If omitted,
 all files in the default directory are listed.

2 Qualifiers
3 /BRIEF
 Displays only file names, without size, date, or other
 details.

3 /FULL
 Displays complete file information including size, date,
 owner, and protection.

3 /SIZE
 Displays file sizes alongside file names.

3 /DATE
 Displays file modification dates.

3 /OWNER
 Displays file owner information.

3 /PROTECTION
 Displays file protection settings.

3 /VERSIONS
 Specifies the version limit for display.

 Format: /VERSIONS=n

3 /TOTAL
 Displays only summary totals (file count and total size)
 without listing individual files.

3 /COLUMNS
 Specifies the number of columns for the display.

 Format: /COLUMNS=n

3 /OUTPUT
 Directs the listing to a file instead of the terminal.

 Format: /OUTPUT=filespec

2 Examples

 $ DIRECTORY
 $ DIRECTORY *.TXT
 $ DIRECTORY/FULL SYS$SYSTEM:*.EXE
 $ DIRECTORY/SIZE/DATE [.SUBDIR]

1 DISMOUNT
 Dismounts a volume from a device.

 Format: DISMOUNT device-name

 Marks the volume as no longer available and removes
 the device assignment.

2 Parameters
3 device-name
 The name of the device to dismount.

2 Examples

 $ DISMOUNT DKA100:

1 DUMP
 Displays the contents of a file in hexadecimal and ASCII format.

 Format: DUMP filespec

 Reads the specified file and displays its contents as both
 hexadecimal values and their ASCII character equivalents.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The file to dump.

2 Qualifiers
3 /BLOCKS
 Specifies a range of blocks to dump.

 Format: /BLOCKS=(START:n,END:m)

3 /BYTE
 Displays data in byte format (default).

3 /WORD
 Displays data in word (16-bit) format.

3 /LONGWORD
 Displays data in longword (32-bit) format.

3 /RECORDS
 Dumps data on a record-by-record basis.

3 /OUTPUT
 Directs the dump output to a file.

 Format: /OUTPUT=filespec

2 Examples

 $ DUMP DATAFILE.DAT
 $ DUMP/BLOCKS=(START:1,END:10) DISK.IMG

1 EDIT
 Invokes the EDT text editor.

 Format: EDIT filespec

 Opens the specified file in the OVMX EDT line editor.
 EDT provides line-oriented editing with commands for
 inserting, deleting, moving, and substituting text.

 EDT Commands:
   TYPE [range]         Display lines
   INSERT [line]        Insert text before a line
   DELETE [range]       Delete lines
   SUBSTITUTE /old/new/ [range]  Replace text
   MOVE range TO line   Move lines
   COPY range TO line   Copy lines
   WRITE [file]         Save the file
   EXIT                 Save and exit
   QUIT                 Exit without saving
   HELP                 Show EDT help

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The file to edit. If the file does not exist, a new file
 is created.

2 Examples

 $ EDIT LOGIN.COM
 $ EDIT SYS$LOGIN:NOTES.TXT

1 EXIT
 Terminates the current command procedure level or session.

 Format: EXIT [status-code]

 In a command procedure, EXIT returns control to the calling
 procedure or to the interactive command level. In interactive
 mode at the top level, EXIT terminates the session.

2 Parameters
3 status-code
 An optional integer status code to return. Odd values
 indicate success, even values indicate error.

2 Examples

 $ EXIT
 $ EXIT 1
 $ EXIT %X10000000

1 HELP
 Displays online help information about OVMX commands,
 qualifiers, parameters, and lexical functions.

 Format: HELP [topic [subtopic [...]]]

 Without arguments, HELP displays a list of available topics.
 You can navigate through help levels:
   - Type a topic name to see its help text
   - Press RETURN for a list of subtopics
   - Type a period (.) to go up one level
   - Press RETURN at the top level to exit

2 Parameters
3 topic
 The name of the help topic to display.

3 subtopic
 Additional subtopic levels, separated by spaces.

2 Examples

 $ HELP
 $ HELP COPY
 $ HELP SET DEFAULT
 $ HELP DIRECTORY Qualifiers /FULL
 $ HELP SHOW TIME

1 INQUIRE
 Reads input from the terminal and assigns it to a symbol.

 Format: INQUIRE symbol-name [prompt-string]

 Prompts the user for input and assigns the response to the
 specified DCL symbol. If the user presses only RETURN, the
 symbol is assigned a null string.

2 Parameters
3 symbol-name
 The name of the symbol to receive the input value.

3 prompt-string
 An optional prompt string to display. If omitted, the
 symbol name followed by a colon is used as the prompt.

2 Qualifiers
3 /NOPUNCTUATION
 Suppresses the default colon and space after the prompt
 string.

3 /GLOBAL
 Creates a global symbol instead of a local symbol.

2 Examples

 $ INQUIRE NAME "Enter your name"
 $ INQUIRE/GLOBAL ANSWER "Continue? (Y/N)"

1 INSTALL
 Manages known images (shared executables).

 Format: INSTALL command [filespec]

 Used to install shared images and manage the known image
 list. Installed images can be shared among multiple processes.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The image file to install or manage.

2 Qualifiers
3 /LIST
 Lists currently installed images.

3 /SHARED
 Installs the image as a shared image.

3 /OPEN
 Installs the image as permanently open.

2 Examples

 $ INSTALL LIST
 $ INSTALL ADD SYS$SYSTEM:LOGINOUT.EXE/SHARED

1 LIBRARY
 Manages text, help, and object module libraries.

 Format: LIBRARY library-file [module-file]

 LIBRARY creates and manages libraries that contain collections
 of text modules, help text, object modules, or macro definitions.

2 Parameters
3 library-file
 The name of the library file to operate on.

3 module-file
 The file containing modules to insert or extract.

2 Qualifiers
3 /CREATE
 Creates a new library file.

 Format: LIBRARY/CREATE library-file

3 /INSERT
 Inserts a module into the library.

 Format: LIBRARY/INSERT library-file module-file

3 /EXTRACT
 Extracts a module from the library.

 Format: LIBRARY/EXTRACT=module library-file

3 /LIST
 Lists the modules in a library.

 Format: LIBRARY/LIST library-file

3 /DELETE
 Removes a module from the library.

 Format: LIBRARY/DELETE=module library-file

3 /HELP
 Specifies that the library is a help library.

3 /TEXT
 Specifies that the library is a text library (default).

3 /REPLACE
 Replaces an existing module in the library.

2 Examples

 $ LIBRARY/CREATE MYLIB.TLB
 $ LIBRARY/INSERT MYLIB.TLB MODULE.TXT
 $ LIBRARY/LIST MYLIB.TLB
 $ LIBRARY/EXTRACT=HEADER MYLIB.TLB

1 LINK
 Links object modules to create an executable image.

 Format: LINK filespec[,...]

 Resolves external references and combines one or more object
 modules into an executable program image.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 One or more object files to link. Default file type is .OBJ.

2 Qualifiers
3 /EXECUTABLE
 Specifies the name of the output executable file.

 Format: /EXECUTABLE=filespec

3 /MAP
 Creates a link map file showing symbol locations.

 Format: /MAP[=filespec]

3 /DEBUG
 Includes debug symbol information in the image.

2 Examples

 $ LINK MYPROG
 $ LINK/EXECUTABLE=MYPROG.EXE MAIN,SUB1,SUB2

1 LOGOUT
 Terminates the current interactive session.

 Format: LOGOUT

 Ends the login session, closes all open files, and
 deassigns all process logical names.

2 Qualifiers
3 /FULL
 Displays accounting information before logging out.

3 /BRIEF
 Suppresses the logout message.

2 Examples

 $ LOGOUT
 $ LOGOUT/FULL

1 MAIL
 Sends and receives electronic mail messages.

 Format: MAIL [filespec] [recipient]

 The MAIL utility provides electronic mail services within
 the OVMX environment. Without arguments, enters interactive
 mail mode.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 A file containing the message to send.

3 recipient
 The username of the mail recipient.

2 Qualifiers
3 /SUBJECT
 Specifies the subject line for the message.

 Format: /SUBJECT="text"

2 Examples

 $ MAIL
 $ MAIL MESSAGE.TXT SMITH /SUBJECT="Meeting notes"

1 MONITOR
 Displays real-time system activity and performance statistics.

 Format: MONITOR [class]

 Shows live system metrics such as CPU usage, memory utilization,
 I/O rates, and process activity.

2 Parameters
3 class
 The class of data to monitor. Available classes include
 SYSTEM, PROCESSES, DISK, and STATES.

2 Examples

 $ MONITOR
 $ MONITOR SYSTEM
 $ MONITOR PROCESSES

1 MOUNT
 Mounts a volume on a device, making it available for access.

 Format: MOUNT device-name [volume-label]

 Associates a physical or virtual device with the system,
 enabling file operations on the mounted volume.

2 Parameters
3 device-name
 The name of the device to mount.

3 volume-label
 An optional label to assign to the volume.

2 Qualifiers
3 /SYSTEM
 Mounts the volume for system-wide access.

3 /FOREIGN
 Mounts the volume as a foreign volume (no file structure
 processing).

2 Examples

 $ MOUNT DKA100: USERDATA
 $ MOUNT/SYSTEM DKA200: SHARED

1 OPEN
 Opens a file for reading or writing.

 Format: OPEN logical-name filespec

 Opens the specified file and assigns a logical name to it.
 The file can then be accessed with READ and WRITE commands
 using the logical name.

2 Parameters
3 logical-name
 The logical name to assign to the opened file.

3 filespec
 The file to open.

2 Qualifiers
3 /READ
 Opens the file for reading only (default).

3 /WRITE
 Opens the file for writing.

3 /APPEND
 Opens the file for appending (write at end).

3 /SHARE
 Allows shared access to the file.

2 Examples

 $ OPEN/READ INFILE DATA.TXT
 $ OPEN/WRITE OUTFILE RESULTS.DAT
 $ OPEN/APPEND LOGFILE ACTIVITY.LOG

1 PHONE
 Provides real-time terminal-to-terminal communication.

 Format: PHONE [username]

 Initiates or answers a phone conversation with another
 user on the system.

2 Parameters
3 username
 The username of the person to call.

2 Examples

 $ PHONE SMITH

1 PIPE
 Executes one or more commands connected by pipes.

 Format: PIPE command1 | command2 [| command3 ...]

 Connects the output of one command to the input of the next,
 similar to Unix-style pipelines. Each command runs as a
 separate subprocess.

2 Parameters
3 command
 One or more DCL commands separated by the pipe character (|).

2 Examples

 $ PIPE DIRECTORY | SEARCH SYS$PIPE ".TXT"
 $ PIPE TYPE FILE.DAT | SORT SYS$PIPE SYS$OUTPUT

1 PRINT
 Queues a file for printing on a print device.

 Format: PRINT filespec[,...]

 Submits the specified file to a print queue for output.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The file or files to print.

2 Qualifiers
3 /COPIES
 Specifies the number of copies to print.

 Format: /COPIES=n

3 /QUEUE
 Specifies the print queue.

 Format: /QUEUE=queue-name

3 /DELETE
 Deletes the file after successful printing.

3 /NOTIFY
 Sends a notification when the print job completes.

3 /FORMS
 Specifies the forms type for printing.

 Format: /FORMS=form-name

2 Examples

 $ PRINT REPORT.TXT
 $ PRINT/COPIES=3 MEMO.TXT
 $ PRINT/QUEUE=SYS$PRINT LISTING.LIS

1 PRODUCT
 Manages software products installed on the system.

 Format: PRODUCT command [product-name]

 Installs, removes, or displays information about layered
 software products.

2 Parameters
3 command
 The product management command: INSTALL, REMOVE, SHOW,
 LIST, or CONFIGURE.

3 product-name
 The name of the product to manage.

2 Examples

 $ PRODUCT SHOW PRODUCT *
 $ PRODUCT INSTALL OVMX

1 PURGE
 Deletes all but the most recent versions of a file.

 Format: PURGE [filespec]

 Removes older versions of files, keeping only the highest
 version number. Without a file specification, purges all
 files in the current directory.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The file or files to purge. Wildcards are permitted.

2 Qualifiers
3 /KEEP
 Specifies the number of versions to keep.

 Format: /KEEP=n

3 /CONFIRM
 Prompts before deleting each version.

3 /LOG
 Displays each file version as it is deleted.

2 Examples

 $ PURGE
 $ PURGE *.TXT
 $ PURGE/KEEP=3 REPORT.DAT

1 READ
 Reads a record from an open file into a symbol.

 Format: READ logical-name symbol-name

 Reads the next record from the file identified by the
 logical name and assigns its contents to the specified
 DCL symbol.

2 Parameters
3 logical-name
 The logical name of the file (from OPEN).

3 symbol-name
 The symbol to receive the record contents.

2 Qualifiers
3 /END_OF_FILE
 Specifies a label to branch to on end-of-file.

 Format: /END_OF_FILE=label

3 /DELETE
 Deletes the record after reading (for indexed files).

2 Examples

 $ OPEN/READ INP DATA.TXT
 $ READ INP LINE
 $ CLOSE INP

1 RECALL
 Displays or re-executes commands from the command history.

 Format: RECALL [command-number]

 Without arguments, displays the command history buffer.
 With a number, re-executes the specified command from
 the history.

2 Parameters
3 command-number
 The number of a command in the history buffer to
 re-execute.

2 Qualifiers
3 /ALL
 Displays the entire command history buffer.

2 Examples

 $ RECALL
 $ RECALL 5
 $ RECALL/ALL

1 RENAME
 Changes the name or location of a file.

 Format: RENAME old-name new-name

 Changes the file specification of an existing file. Can be
 used to move files between directories on the same device.

2 Parameters
3 old-name
 The current file specification.

3 new-name
 The new file specification.

2 Qualifiers
3 /CONFIRM
 Prompts before each rename.

3 /LOG
 Displays each file as it is renamed.

2 Examples

 $ RENAME OLDNAME.TXT NEWNAME.TXT
 $ RENAME FILE.DAT [.ARCHIVE]FILE.DAT
 $ RENAME/LOG *.TMP *.BAK

1 REPLY
 Sends an operator reply message or enables/disables an
 operator terminal.

 Format: REPLY ["message"]
         REPLY/ENABLE[=class]
         REPLY/DISABLE[=class]

 Used by operators to respond to user REQUEST messages and
 to manage operator terminal designations.

2 Parameters
3 message
 The text of the reply message, enclosed in quotes.

2 Qualifiers
3 /ENABLE
 Enables the current terminal as an operator terminal.

 Format: /ENABLE[=class]

3 /DISABLE
 Disables operator status for the current terminal.

3 /TO
 Directs the reply to a specific request.

 Format: /TO=request-id

2 Examples

 $ REPLY/ENABLE
 $ REPLY "Backup will begin at 22:00"
 $ REPLY/TO=5 "Request approved"

1 REQUEST
 Sends a message to the system operator.

 Format: REQUEST "message"

 Sends a request message to all enabled operator terminals.
 The operator can respond using the REPLY command.

2 Parameters
3 message
 The text of the request message, enclosed in quotes.

2 Qualifiers
3 /TO
 Specifies a particular operator class to receive the
 request.

 Format: /TO=class

2 Examples

 $ REQUEST "Please mount tape MTA0:"
 $ REQUEST/TO=TAPES "Need tape volume BACKUP_01"

1 RUN
 Executes a program image.

 Format: RUN filespec

 Loads and executes the specified program image. The default
 file type is .EXE.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The executable image to run.

2 Examples

 $ RUN MYPROG
 $ RUN SYS$SYSTEM:AUTHORIZE

1 SEARCH
 Searches one or more files for a specified text string.

 Format: SEARCH filespec search-string

 Reads through the specified file(s) and displays each line
 that contains the search string.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The file or files to search. Wildcards are permitted.

3 search-string
 The text string to search for, enclosed in quotes.

2 Qualifiers
3 /EXACT
 Performs a case-sensitive search. By default, the search
 is case-insensitive.

3 /LOG
 Displays the name of each file searched.

3 /NUMBERS
 Displays line numbers alongside matching lines.

3 /OUTPUT
 Directs the search output to a file.

 Format: /OUTPUT=filespec

3 /STATISTICS
 Displays statistics about the search (files searched,
 lines matched).

3 /WINDOW
 Specifies the number of context lines to display before
 and after each match.

 Format: /WINDOW=(BEFORE:n,AFTER:m)

2 Examples

 $ SEARCH REPORT.TXT "error"
 $ SEARCH *.COM "BACKUP"
 $ SEARCH/EXACT CONFIG.DAT "SystemRoot"

1 SET
 Sets or modifies process, system, and file characteristics.

 Format: SET parameter [value]

 The SET command modifies a variety of system, process, and
 file attributes. Each SET option is described under its own
 subtopic.

2 ACCOUNTING
 Enables or modifies process accounting.

 Format: SET ACCOUNTING [/qualifier]

3 /ENABLE
 Enables accounting data collection.

3 /DISABLE
 Disables accounting data collection.

2 AUDIT
 Enables or configures security auditing.

 Format: SET AUDIT [/qualifier]

3 /ENABLE
 Enables security audit events.

3 /DISABLE
 Disables security audit events.

2 CONTROL
 Enables or disables Ctrl-Y and Ctrl-T handling.

 Format: SET CONTROL[=T]
         SET NOCONTROL[=Y]

 SET CONTROL enables Ctrl-Y interrupts (default).
 SET NOCONTROL disables Ctrl-Y interrupts.
 SET CONTROL=T enables Ctrl-T status line.

2 DEFAULT
 Sets the default directory for file operations.

 Format: SET DEFAULT directory-spec

 Changes the current default directory. All subsequent file
 operations that do not specify a directory will use this
 directory.

3 Examples

 $ SET DEFAULT SYS$SYSDEVICE:[USERS.SMITH]
 $ SET DEFAULT [.SUBDIR]
 $ SET DEFAULT [-]

2 ENTRY
 Modifies attributes of a batch or print queue entry.

 Format: SET ENTRY entry-number [/qualifier]

3 /PRIORITY
 Changes the priority of a queue entry.

3 /RELEASE
 Releases a held entry for processing.

2 FILE
 Modifies file attributes.

 Format: SET FILE filespec [/qualifier]

3 /VERSION_LIMIT
 Sets the version limit for the file.

 Format: /VERSION_LIMIT=n

3 /PROTECTION
 Sets file protection.

 Format: /PROTECTION=(S:RWED,O:RWED,G:RE,W)

2 HOST
 Sets the system host name.

 Format: SET HOST hostname

 Establishes or changes the host name of the system.

2 MESSAGE
 Controls the display of system message components.

 Format: SET MESSAGE [/qualifier]

 Controls which parts of system messages are displayed:
 facility name, severity, identification, and text.

3 /FACILITY
 Controls display of the facility prefix.

3 /SEVERITY
 Controls display of the severity level.

3 /IDENTIFICATION
 Controls display of the message identifier.

3 /TEXT
 Controls display of the message text.

3 /NOFACILITY
 Suppresses the facility prefix.

3 /NOSEVERITY
 Suppresses the severity level.

3 /NOIDENTIFICATION
 Suppresses the message identifier.

3 /NOTEXT
 Suppresses the message text.

2 NOON
 Suppresses the ON ERROR action at the current command level.

 Format: SET NOON

 Prevents ON ERROR THEN GOTO or ON ERROR THEN EXIT from
 executing when a command returns an error status. The ON
 ERROR handler remains defined but is not triggered.

2 ON
 Re-enables the ON ERROR action after SET NOON.

 Format: SET ON

 Reverses the effect of SET NOON, allowing the ON ERROR
 handler to fire again on error status.

2 PASSWORD
 Changes the password for the current account.

 Format: SET PASSWORD

 Prompts for the old password, then for the new password
 (entered twice for verification).

2 PROCESS
 Sets process characteristics.

 Format: SET PROCESS [/qualifier]

3 /NAME
 Changes the process name.

 Format: /NAME=new-name

3 /PRIORITY
 Sets the process base priority.

 Format: /PRIORITY=n

2 PROMPT
 Changes the DCL command prompt.

 Format: SET PROMPT string

 Changes the prompt displayed by DCL. The string can include
 spaces if enclosed in quotes.

3 Examples

 $ SET PROMPT "OVMX> "
 $ SET PROMPT "''F$TIME()' $ "

2 PROTECTION
 Sets the default file protection.

 Format: SET PROTECTION=(code)/DEFAULT
         SET PROTECTION=(code) filespec

 The protection code specifies access for System, Owner,
 Group, and World categories, each with Read, Write,
 Execute, and Delete permissions.

3 Examples

 $ SET PROTECTION=(S:RWED,O:RWED,G:RE,W:E)/DEFAULT
 $ SET PROTECTION=(S:RWED,O:RWED,G,W) PRIVATE.DAT

2 TERMINAL
 Sets terminal characteristics.

 Format: SET TERMINAL [device] [/qualifier]

3 /WIDTH
 Sets the terminal display width in columns.

 Format: /WIDTH=n

3 /PAGE
 Sets the terminal page length in lines.

 Format: /PAGE=n

3 /ECHO
 Enables character echo (default).

3 /NOECHO
 Disables character echo.

3 /WRAP
 Enables line wrapping at the terminal width.

3 /NOWRAP
 Disables line wrapping.

3 /INSERT
 Sets insert mode for line editing.

3 /OVERSTRIKE
 Sets overstrike mode for line editing.

3 Examples

 $ SET TERMINAL/WIDTH=132
 $ SET TERMINAL/PAGE=48

2 TIME
 Sets the system date and time.

 Format: SET TIME=dd-mmm-yyyy:hh:mm:ss

 Requires OPER or SYSPRV privilege.

2 UIC
 Sets the UIC (User Identification Code) for the current
 process.

 Format: SET UIC [group,member]

2 VERIFY
 Enables or disables command procedure verification.

 Format: SET VERIFY
         SET NOVERIFY

 When verification is enabled, each command in a command
 procedure is displayed before it is executed.

3 Examples

 $ SET VERIFY
 $ SET NOVERIFY

2 VOLUME
 Sets volume attributes for a mounted VMSFS volume.

 Format: SET VOLUME device [/qualifier]

 Requires a mounted VMSFS filesystem.

2 WORKING_SET
 Sets working set parameters for the current process.

 Format: SET WORKING_SET [/qualifier]

3 /QUOTA
 Sets the working set quota.

 Format: /QUOTA=n

3 /EXTENT
 Sets the working set extent.

 Format: /EXTENT=n

3 /LIMIT
 Sets the working set limit.

 Format: /LIMIT=n

1 SHOW
 Displays information about the system, process, or files.

 Format: SHOW item [options]

 The SHOW command displays various types of system and process
 information. Each SHOW option is described under its own
 subtopic.

2 ACCOUNTING
 Displays accounting information for the current session.

 Format: SHOW ACCOUNTING

 Displays session start time, elapsed time, CPU time used,
 and other resource utilization statistics.

2 AUDIT
 Displays security audit configuration.

 Format: SHOW AUDIT

 Shows which security audit events are currently enabled.

2 CLUSTER
 Displays cluster membership information.

 Format: SHOW CLUSTER

 Shows the systems that are members of the VMScluster.

2 DEFAULT
 Displays the current default directory.

 Format: SHOW DEFAULT

 Shows the directory specification used as the default for
 file operations.

3 Examples

 $ SHOW DEFAULT
   SYS$SYSDEVICE:[USERS.SMITH]

2 DEVICE
 Displays information about mounted devices.

 Format: SHOW DEVICE [device-name]

 Lists mounted filesystems and their characteristics,
 displayed as VMS device names. Without an argument,
 shows all mounted devices.

3 /FULL
 Shows extended device characteristics including
 volume label, free space, and mount status.

3 Examples

 $ SHOW DEVICE
 $ SHOW DEVICE DKA0:

2 ENTRY
 Displays information about batch or print queue entries.

 Format: SHOW ENTRY [entry-number]

2 ERROR
 Displays information about the last error that occurred.

 Format: SHOW ERROR

 Shows the error count and details of recent errors.

2 INTRUSION
 Displays the intrusion detection database.

 Format: SHOW INTRUSION

 Shows records of failed login attempts and suspected
 break-in activity.

2 LICENSE
 Displays active product licenses.

 Format: SHOW LICENSE

 Lists the software licenses registered on the system.

2 LOGICAL
 Displays logical name definitions.

 Format: SHOW LOGICAL [logical-name]

 Without an argument, displays all logical names in the
 process table. With an argument, translates the specified
 logical name and shows its equivalence string.

3 /TABLE
 Specifies the logical name table to search.

 Format: /TABLE=table-name

3 /FULL
 Shows the full translation chain, including iterative
 translations.

3 Examples

 $ SHOW LOGICAL
 $ SHOW LOGICAL SYS$LOGIN
 $ SHOW LOGICAL/TABLE=LNM$SYSTEM *

2 MEMORY
 Displays system memory utilization.

 Format: SHOW MEMORY

 Shows total physical memory, available memory, and
 memory usage breakdown.

3 /FULL
 Displays detailed memory pool statistics.

2 NETWORK
 Displays network configuration and status.

 Format: SHOW NETWORK

 Shows network interface information and connectivity
 status.

2 PROCESS
 Displays information about the current process.

 Format: SHOW PROCESS [process-name]

 Shows process identification, priority, state, and
 resource utilization.

3 /PRIVILEGES
 Displays the privileges held by the process.

3 /QUOTAS
 Displays the process resource quotas.

3 /ALL
 Displays all available process information.

3 Examples

 $ SHOW PROCESS
 $ SHOW PROCESS/PRIVILEGES
 $ SHOW PROCESS/QUOTAS

2 PROTECTION
 Displays the current default file protection.

 Format: SHOW PROTECTION

 Shows the default protection mask applied to newly
 created files.

3 Examples

 $ SHOW PROTECTION
   SYSTEM=RWED, OWNER=RWED, GROUP=RE, WORLD=NO ACCESS

2 QUEUE
 Displays information about batch and print queues.

 Format: SHOW QUEUE [queue-name]

 Lists the queues defined on the system and their
 current entries.

3 /ALL
 Shows all entries in the specified queue.

3 /FULL
 Shows detailed queue and entry information.

2 QUOTA
 Displays disk quota information.

 Format: SHOW QUOTA

 Shows disk space quota and current usage for the
 current user.

2 ROOT
 Displays the system root directory.

 Format: SHOW ROOT

 Shows the top-level system directory specification.

2 STATUS
 Displays the status of the last command executed.

 Format: SHOW STATUS

 Shows the exit status code and condition value from
 the most recently executed command or program.

2 SYMBOL
 Displays the value of a DCL symbol.

 Format: SHOW SYMBOL symbol-name

 Translates and displays the specified symbol. Shows both
 the symbol value and its type (string or integer).

3 /ALL
 Displays all defined symbols.

3 /GLOBAL
 Displays global symbols only.

3 /LOCAL
 Displays local symbols only.

3 Examples

 $ SHOW SYMBOL *
 $ SHOW SYMBOL STATUS

2 SYSTEM
 Displays information about all processes on the system.

 Format: SHOW SYSTEM

 Lists all active processes with their PID, username,
 state, priority, and resource consumption.

3 /FULL
 Displays extended process information.

2 TERMINAL
 Displays terminal characteristics.

 Format: SHOW TERMINAL [device]

 Shows the current settings for the terminal, including
 width, page size, and editing mode.

2 TIME
 Displays the current date and time.

 Format: SHOW TIME

 Displays the date and time in VMS format:
 DD-MMM-YYYY HH:MM:SS.CC

3 Examples

 $ SHOW TIME
   5-MAR-2026 14:30:22.15

2 TRANSLATION
 Translates a logical name and shows the full chain.

 Format: SHOW TRANSLATION logical-name

 Iteratively translates the logical name, showing each
 step in the translation chain until the final equivalence
 string is reached.

3 Examples

 $ SHOW TRANSLATION SYS$SYSTEM

2 USERS
 Displays information about logged-in users.

 Format: SHOW USERS

 Lists all users currently logged in to the system.

2 VERIFY
 Displays the current verification setting.

 Format: SHOW VERIFY

 Shows whether command procedure verification is
 currently enabled or disabled.

2 WORKING_SET
 Displays the current working set parameters.

 Format: SHOW WORKING_SET

 Shows the working set quota, extent, and current
 size for the process.

1 SORT
 Sorts records in a file and writes the result.

 Format: SORT input-file output-file

 Reads the input file, sorts the records, and writes the
 sorted output to the specified output file.

2 Parameters
3 input-file
 The file containing records to sort.

3 output-file
 The file to receive the sorted records.

2 Qualifiers
3 /KEY
 Specifies the sort key position and size.

 Format: /KEY=(POSITION:n,SIZE:m)

3 /DUPLICATE
 Controls handling of duplicate records.

3 /STABLE
 Preserves the original order of records with equal keys.

2 Examples

 $ SORT UNSORTED.DAT SORTED.DAT
 $ SORT/KEY=(POSITION:1,SIZE:20) DATA.TXT RESULT.TXT

1 SPAWN
 Creates a subprocess.

 Format: SPAWN [command]

 Creates a subprocess of the current process. Without an
 argument, starts an interactive subprocess. With a command
 argument, executes the command in a subprocess and returns.

2 Parameters
3 command
 An optional command to execute in the subprocess.

2 Qualifiers
3 /NOWAIT
 Returns control immediately without waiting for the
 subprocess to complete.

3 /NOLOGICAL_NAMES
 Does not pass logical names to the subprocess.

3 /NOSYMBOLS
 Does not pass symbols to the subprocess.

3 /OUTPUT
 Directs subprocess output to a file.

 Format: /OUTPUT=filespec

3 /PROCESS
 Assigns a name to the subprocess.

 Format: /PROCESS=name

2 Examples

 $ SPAWN
 $ SPAWN DIRECTORY *.TXT
 $ SPAWN/NOWAIT BACKUP [...]*.* SAVE.BCK

1 STOP
 Stops the current process or a specified process.

 Format: STOP [process-name]
         STOP/ID=pid

 Terminates execution of a process. Without arguments,
 stops the current process.

2 Parameters
3 process-name
 The name of the process to stop.

2 Qualifiers
3 /ID
 Specifies the process by PID.

 Format: /ID=hex-pid

2 Examples

 $ STOP
 $ STOP BATCH_JOB
 $ STOP/ID=20400123

1 SUBMIT
 Submits a command procedure to a batch queue for execution.

 Format: SUBMIT filespec

 Places the specified command procedure in a batch queue
 for asynchronous execution.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The command procedure file to submit. Default file
 type is .COM.

2 Qualifiers
3 /QUEUE
 Specifies the batch queue.

 Format: /QUEUE=queue-name

3 /AFTER
 Specifies a time after which the job should execute.

 Format: /AFTER=time

3 /LOG_FILE
 Specifies the log file for batch job output.

 Format: /LOG_FILE=filespec

3 /NOTIFY
 Sends notification when the batch job completes.

3 /PRIORITY
 Sets the job priority.

 Format: /PRIORITY=n

3 /NAME
 Assigns a name to the job.

 Format: /NAME=job-name

2 Examples

 $ SUBMIT NIGHTLY.COM
 $ SUBMIT/AFTER=22:00 BACKUP.COM
 $ SUBMIT/QUEUE=SYS$BATCH/NOTIFY REPORT.COM

1 SYSGEN
 System generation utility.

 Format: SYSGEN

 Displays or modifies system parameters that control
 the operating environment. In OVMX, SYSGEN provides
 read-only access to system parameter values.

2 Examples

 $ RUN SYS$SYSTEM:SYSGEN
 SYSGEN> SHOW MAXPROCESSCNT

1 SYSMAN
 System management utility.

 Format: SYSMAN

 Provides system management functions for cluster-wide
 operations. In OVMX, SYSMAN provides basic local system
 management capabilities.

2 Examples

 $ RUN SYS$SYSTEM:SYSMAN

1 TCPIP
 TCP/IP Services network management commands.

 Format: TCPIP subcommand

 Provides commands for managing TCP/IP network services,
 including interface configuration, route management,
 and service control.

2 SHOW
 Displays TCP/IP network information.

3 INTERFACE
 Shows network interface status and configuration.

 Format: TCPIP SHOW INTERFACE

3 ROUTE
 Displays the routing table.

 Format: TCPIP SHOW ROUTE

3 VERSION
 Displays the TCP/IP Services version.

 Format: TCPIP SHOW VERSION

3 HOST
 Displays host name information.

 Format: TCPIP SHOW HOST hostname

2 SET
 Configures TCP/IP parameters.

3 INTERFACE
 Configures a network interface.

 Format: TCPIP SET INTERFACE iface /ADDRESS=addr

3 HOST
 Sets host table entries.

 Format: TCPIP SET HOST hostname /ADDRESS=addr

2 Examples

 $ TCPIP SHOW INTERFACE
 $ TCPIP SHOW ROUTE
 $ TCPIP SHOW VERSION

1 TYPE
 Displays the contents of a file on the terminal.

 Format: TYPE filespec

 Reads the specified file and displays each record on
 the terminal.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The file to display.

2 Qualifiers
3 /PAGE
 Pauses the display after each screenful of output.
 Press RETURN to continue.

3 /OUTPUT
 Directs the output to a file instead of the terminal.

 Format: /OUTPUT=filespec

2 Examples

 $ TYPE LOGIN.COM
 $ TYPE/PAGE LONGFILE.TXT
 $ TYPE SYS$MANAGER:SYLOGIN.COM

1 WAIT
 Suspends process execution for a specified time interval.

 Format: WAIT delta-time

 Pauses the current process for the specified duration.
 The time format is HH:MM:SS or a number of seconds.

2 Parameters
3 delta-time
 The time interval to wait. Format is HH:MM:SS or
 a number of seconds.

2 Examples

 $ WAIT 00:00:05
 $ WAIT 00:01:00

1 WRITE
 Writes a record to an open file or to a logical name.

 Format: WRITE logical-name expression[,...]

 Writes the value of the expression(s) to the file
 identified by the logical name. SYS$OUTPUT writes
 to the terminal.

2 Parameters
3 logical-name
 The logical name of the file (from OPEN) or SYS$OUTPUT
 for terminal output.

3 expression
 The data to write. Can be a quoted string, a symbol name,
 or an expression combining these with the + operator.

2 Qualifiers
3 /SYMBOL
 Writes the contents of a symbol, expanding it fully.

2 Examples

 $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Hello, World!"
 $ OPEN/WRITE OUTF RESULTS.DAT
 $ WRITE OUTF "Line of data"
 $ CLOSE OUTF

1 F$CONTEXT
 Sets or resets the process selection context for F$PID.

 Format: F$CONTEXT(context_type, context_symbol, selection, value, match)

 Establishes criteria for selecting processes with F$PID.
 Use before calling F$PID in a loop.

2 Parameters
3 context_type
 The type of context: "PROCESS" is the only valid type.

3 context_symbol
 A symbol name to hold the context state.

3 selection
 The selection criterion: USERNAME, NODENAME, etc.

3 value
 The value to match against.

3 match
 The match type: EQL or NEQ.

2 Examples

 $ x = F$CONTEXT("PROCESS", ctx, "USERNAME", "SMITH", "EQL")

1 F$CVSI
 Extracts a bit field from a character string and returns it
 as a signed integer.

 Format: F$CVSI(bit_position, size, source)

2 Parameters
3 bit_position
 The starting bit position (0-based).

3 size
 The number of bits to extract.

3 source
 The source string.

2 Examples

 $ val = F$CVSI(0, 8, str)

1 F$CVTIME
 Converts or extracts components from a date/time string.

 Format: F$CVTIME([time_string [, output_format [, field]]])

 Without arguments, returns the current date/time. With
 arguments, converts between time formats or extracts
 specific fields.

2 Parameters
3 time_string
 The date/time string to convert. If empty or omitted,
 uses the current time.

3 output_format
 The output format: ABSOLUTE, COMPARISON, or DELTA.
 Default is ABSOLUTE.

3 field
 The field to extract: YEAR, MONTH, DAY, HOUR, MINUTE,
 SECOND, WEEKDAY, DATE, TIME, DATETIME.

2 Examples

 $ day = F$CVTIME(,,"WEEKDAY")
 $ year = F$CVTIME(,,"YEAR")
 $ t = F$CVTIME("15-JAN-2025","COMPARISON","DATE")

1 F$CVUI
 Extracts a bit field from a character string and returns it
 as an unsigned integer.

 Format: F$CVUI(bit_position, size, source)

2 Parameters
3 bit_position
 The starting bit position (0-based).

3 size
 The number of bits to extract.

3 source
 The source string.

2 Examples

 $ val = F$CVUI(0, 16, str)

1 F$DEVICE
 Returns the next device name matching a search pattern.

 Format: F$DEVICE(search_name, device_class, device_type)

 Iterative function that returns one device name per call.
 Returns an empty string when no more devices match.

2 Parameters
3 search_name
 Wildcard device name pattern.

3 device_class
 The class of device to match.

3 device_type
 The type of device to match.

2 Examples

 $ dev = F$DEVICE("DK*","DISK","")

1 F$DIRECTORY
 Returns the current default directory in VMS format.

 Format: F$DIRECTORY()

 Equivalent to F$ENVIRONMENT("DEFAULT"). Returns the
 current directory specification as a VMS-style string.

2 Examples

 $ dir = F$DIRECTORY()
 $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Default is ''F$DIRECTORY()'"

1 F$EDIT
 Performs string editing operations.

 Format: F$EDIT(string, edit_list)

 Applies one or more editing operations to the input string
 and returns the result.

2 Parameters
3 string
 The string to edit.

3 edit_list
 A comma-separated list of editing operations:
   COLLAPSE  - Remove all spaces and tabs
   COMPRESS  - Replace multiple spaces/tabs with one space
   LOWERCASE - Convert to lowercase
   TRIM      - Remove leading and trailing spaces
   UNCOMMENT - Remove text after ! comment delimiter
   UPCASE    - Convert to uppercase

2 Examples

 $ s = F$EDIT("  Hello  World  ", "TRIM,COMPRESS")
 $ u = F$EDIT(name, "UPCASE")
 $ c = F$EDIT(line, "UNCOMMENT,TRIM")

1 F$ELEMENT
 Extracts a delimited element from a string.

 Format: F$ELEMENT(element_number, delimiter, string)

 Splits the string at each occurrence of the delimiter and
 returns the specified element (zero-based). If the element
 number is beyond the last element, returns the delimiter.

2 Parameters
3 element_number
 The zero-based index of the element to extract.

3 delimiter
 The delimiter character used to separate elements.

3 string
 The string containing delimited elements.

2 Examples

 $ second = F$ELEMENT(1, ",", "A,B,C")   ! Returns "B"
 $ part = F$ELEMENT(0, " ", "HELLO WORLD")

1 F$ENVIRONMENT
 Returns information about the DCL environment.

 Format: F$ENVIRONMENT(item)

 Returns the value of the specified environment item.

2 Parameters
3 item
 The environment attribute to query. Valid items:
   DEFAULT      - Current default directory
   DEPTH        - Current command procedure nesting depth
   INTERACTIVE  - Whether session is interactive (TRUE/FALSE)
   KEY_STATE    - Current keypad state
   MAX_DEPTH    - Maximum command procedure depth
   MESSAGE      - Current message format settings
   ON_CONTROL_Y - Current Ctrl-Y action setting
   ON_SEVERITY  - Current ON severity setting
   PROMPT       - Current DCL prompt string
   PROCEDURE    - Current command procedure name
   SYMBOL_SCOPE - Current symbol scope
   VERB_SCOPE   - Current verb scope
   VERIFY       - Verification setting (0 or 1)

2 Examples

 $ dir = F$ENVIRONMENT("DEFAULT")
 $ IF F$ENVIRONMENT("INTERACTIVE") THEN GOTO INTER
 $ depth = F$ENVIRONMENT("DEPTH")

1 F$EXTRACT
 Extracts a substring from a string.

 Format: F$EXTRACT(start, length, string)

 Returns a substring beginning at the specified position
 for the specified length. Positions are zero-based.

2 Parameters
3 start
 The zero-based starting position.

3 length
 The number of characters to extract.

3 string
 The source string.

2 Examples

 $ mid = F$EXTRACT(3, 5, "ABCDEFGHIJ")  ! Returns "DEFGH"
 $ first = F$EXTRACT(0, 1, name)

1 F$FAO
 Performs formatted ASCII output conversion.

 Format: F$FAO(control_string, arg1, arg2, ...)

 Converts values to formatted text using a control string
 with formatting directives.

2 Parameters
3 control_string
 The format control string containing FAO directives:
   !AS  - ASCII string
   !SL  - Signed longword (decimal)
   !UL  - Unsigned longword (decimal)
   !XL  - Longword (hexadecimal)
   !OL  - Longword (octal)
   !ZL  - Zero-filled longword
   !/   - Newline
   !_   - Tab
   !^   - Form feed
   !!   - Literal exclamation mark
   !n*c - Repeat character c, n times

3 arg1, arg2, ...
 Arguments consumed by the formatting directives.

2 Examples

 $ s = F$FAO("PID=!XL, Name=!AS", pid, name)
 $ line = F$FAO("Count: !SL!/Status: !AS", n, "OK")

1 F$FILE_ATTRIBUTES
 Returns an attribute of a file.

 Format: F$FILE_ATTRIBUTES(filespec, item)

 Queries the specified file and returns the requested
 attribute value.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The file to query.

3 item
 The attribute to retrieve. Valid items:
   ALQ  - Allocation quantity (blocks)
   BDT  - Backup date/time
   BKS  - Bucket size
   BLS  - Block size
   CBT  - Contiguous best try
   CDT  - Creation date/time
   DEQ  - Default extension quantity
   DID  - Directory ID
   DVI  - Device name
   EDT  - Expiration date/time
   EOF  - End of file block
   FID  - File ID
   FSZ  - Fixed control area size
   GRP  - File group number
   KNOWN - Whether file is a known image
   LRL  - Longest record length
   MBM  - Member number
   MRN  - Maximum record number
   MRS  - Maximum record size
   NOA  - Number of areas
   NOK  - Number of keys
   ORG  - File organization (SEQ, REL, IDX)
   PRO  - File protection
   PVN  - Prologue version number
   RAT  - Record attributes
   RCK  - Read check enabled
   RDT  - Revision date/time
   RFM  - Record format (VAR, FIX, VFC, UDF, STM, STMLF, STMCR)
   RVN  - Revision number
   SIZE - File size in bytes
   UIC  - File owner UIC
   WCK  - Write check enabled

2 Examples

 $ size = F$FILE_ATTRIBUTES("LOGIN.COM", "SIZE")
 $ org = F$FILE_ATTRIBUTES("DATA.DAT", "ORG")
 $ cdt = F$FILE_ATTRIBUTES("PROG.C", "CDT")

1 F$GETDVI
 Returns information about a device.

 Format: F$GETDVI(device_name, item)

 Queries the specified device and returns the requested
 attribute.

2 Parameters
3 device_name
 The device to query.

3 item
 The device attribute to retrieve (DEVCLASS, DEVTYPE,
 FULLDEVNAM, FREEBLOCKS, MAXBLOCK, VOLNAM, etc.).

2 Examples

 $ class = F$GETDVI("SYS$SYSDEVICE", "DEVCLASS")

1 F$GETJPI
 Returns information about a process.

 Format: F$GETJPI(pid, item)

 Queries the specified process and returns the requested
 information item.

2 Parameters
3 pid
 The process ID, or empty string for the current process.

3 item
 The process attribute to retrieve. Valid items:
   PRCNAM    - Process name
   USERNAME  - Username
   UIC       - User Identification Code
   PID       - Process ID (hex)
   GRP       - Group number
   MEM       - Member number
   MASTER_PID - Master process ID
   OWNER     - Owner process ID
   STS       - Process status flags
   PRI       - Current priority
   PRIB      - Base priority
   STATE     - Process state
   CPUTIM    - CPU time used (hundredths)
   PAGEFLTS  - Page faults
   PPGCNT    - Process page count
   GPGCNT    - Global page count
   WSSIZE    - Working set size
   DFWSCNT   - Default working set size
   WSQUOTA   - Working set quota
   WSEXTENT  - Working set extent
   VIRTPEAK  - Peak virtual size
   DIRIO     - Direct I/O count
   BUFIO     - Buffered I/O count
   TERMINAL  - Terminal name
   MODE      - Process mode
   IMAGNAME  - Current image name
   LOGINTIM  - Login time

2 Examples

 $ name = F$GETJPI("", "PRCNAM")
 $ cpu = F$GETJPI("", "CPUTIM")

1 F$GETQUI
 Returns information about a batch or print queue.

 Format: F$GETQUI(function, item, object_id, flags)

 Queries the queue database for information about queues,
 jobs, and entries.

2 Parameters
3 function
 CANCEL_OPERATION, DISPLAY_ENTRY, DISPLAY_JOB,
 DISPLAY_QUEUE, etc.

3 item
 The attribute to return.

3 object_id
 The queue, job, or entry identifier.

3 flags
 Search flags.

2 Examples

 $ queue = F$GETQUI("DISPLAY_QUEUE","QUEUE_NAME","SYS$BATCH")

1 F$GETSYI
 Returns system information.

 Format: F$GETSYI(item)

 Queries system attributes and returns the requested
 information.

2 Parameters
3 item
 The system attribute to retrieve. Valid items:
   ARCH_NAME   - Architecture name (x86_64, aarch64)
   ARCH_TYPE   - Architecture type number
   BOOTTIME    - System boot time
   CHARACTER_EMULATED - Character emulation (FALSE)
   CLUSTER_MEMBER - Cluster membership (FALSE)
   CLUSTER_NODES  - Number of cluster nodes
   CLUSTER_FTIME  - Cluster failover time
   CPU          - CPU type string
   DECIMAL_EMULATED - Decimal emulation (FALSE)
   FLOAT_EMULATED  - Float emulation (FALSE)
   HW_NAME     - Hardware name
   MAXPROCESSCNT - Maximum process count
   MEMSIZE     - Physical memory size (pages)
   NODENAME    - System node name
   NODE_CSID   - Cluster system ID
   NODE_NUMBER - Node number
   PAGE_SIZE   - System page size (bytes)
   PAGEFILE_FREE - Free page file pages
   PAGEFILE_PAGE - Page file size
   SID         - System ID
   VERSION     - Operating system version
   AVAILCPU_CNT - Available CPU count

2 Examples

 $ node = F$GETSYI("NODENAME")
 $ ver = F$GETSYI("VERSION")
 $ arch = F$GETSYI("ARCH_NAME")
 $ mem = F$GETSYI("MEMSIZE")

1 F$IDENTIFIER
 Translates between identifier names and values.

 Format: F$IDENTIFIER(id, conversion_type)

 Converts between identifier names (like usernames) and
 their numeric UIC values.

2 Parameters
3 id
 The identifier name or numeric value.

3 conversion_type
 NAME_TO_NUMBER or NUMBER_TO_NAME.

2 Examples

 $ uic = F$IDENTIFIER("SYSTEM", "NAME_TO_NUMBER")

1 F$INTEGER
 Converts a string expression to an integer value.

 Format: F$INTEGER(expression)

 Evaluates the expression and returns its integer value.
 String representations of numbers are converted.

2 Parameters
3 expression
 The expression to convert to integer.

2 Examples

 $ n = F$INTEGER("42")
 $ x = F$INTEGER(symbol_name)

1 F$LENGTH
 Returns the length of a string.

 Format: F$LENGTH(string)

 Returns the number of characters in the specified string.

2 Parameters
3 string
 The string whose length is to be returned.

2 Examples

 $ len = F$LENGTH("Hello")         ! Returns 5
 $ sz = F$LENGTH(my_symbol)

1 F$LOCATE
 Locates a substring within a string.

 Format: F$LOCATE(substring, string)

 Returns the zero-based position of the first occurrence of
 the substring within the string. If the substring is not
 found, returns the length of the string.

2 Parameters
3 substring
 The text to search for.

3 string
 The string to search within.

2 Examples

 $ pos = F$LOCATE("XY", "ABCXYZ")  ! Returns 3
 $ IF F$LOCATE("ERR", line) .NE. F$LENGTH(line) THEN -
   WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Found error"

1 F$MESSAGE
 Returns the message text for a system status code.

 Format: F$MESSAGE(status_code)

 Translates a numeric system status code into its
 corresponding message text.

2 Parameters
3 status_code
 The numeric status code to translate.

2 Examples

 $ msg = F$MESSAGE(%X10000001)
 $ txt = F$MESSAGE($STATUS)

1 F$MODE
 Returns the mode of the current process.

 Format: F$MODE()

 Returns a string indicating the mode: INTERACTIVE, BATCH,
 NETWORK, or OTHER.

2 Examples

 $ mode = F$MODE()
 $ IF F$MODE() .EQS. "INTERACTIVE" THEN GOTO INTER_SETUP

1 F$PARSE
 Parses a file specification and returns a component.

 Format: F$PARSE(filespec [, default [, related [, field]]])

 Parses the file specification using default and related
 specifications, then returns the requested field or the
 fully expanded specification.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The file specification to parse.

3 default
 Default file specification values.

3 related
 Related file specification for context.

3 field
 The field to return: NODE, DEVICE, DIRECTORY, NAME,
 TYPE, VERSION, or empty for the full specification.

2 Examples

 $ dev = F$PARSE("LOGIN.COM",,,"DEVICE")
 $ dir = F$PARSE("FILE.TXT",,,"DIRECTORY")
 $ full = F$PARSE("PROG","SYS$SYSTEM:.EXE")

1 F$PID
 Returns the next process ID in the system.

 Format: F$PID(context_symbol)

 Iterative function that returns one PID per call.
 Returns an empty string when no more processes match.
 Use F$CONTEXT to set selection criteria.

2 Parameters
3 context_symbol
 A symbol name holding the iteration context. Set to
 empty string to start a new scan.

2 Examples

 $ pid = F$PID(ctx)
 $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT F$GETJPI(pid, "PRCNAM")

1 F$PRIVILEGE
 Checks whether the current process holds specified privileges.

 Format: F$PRIVILEGE(privilege_list)

 Returns TRUE if the process holds all of the specified
 privileges, FALSE otherwise.

2 Parameters
3 privilege_list
 A comma-separated list of privilege names to check.
 Prefix with NO to check that a privilege is NOT held.
 Valid privileges include: TMPMBX, NETMBX, SYSPRV,
 BYPASS, OPER, WORLD, GROUP, CMKRNL, SYSNAM, etc.

2 Examples

 $ IF F$PRIVILEGE("SYSPRV") THEN GOTO PRIV_OPS
 $ ok = F$PRIVILEGE("TMPMBX,NETMBX")

1 F$PROCESS
 Returns the process name of the current process.

 Format: F$PROCESS()

 Returns the name assigned to the current process.

2 Examples

 $ name = F$PROCESS()
 $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Process: ''F$PROCESS()'"

1 F$SEARCH
 Searches for files matching a wildcard specification.

 Format: F$SEARCH(filespec)

 Iterative function that returns one matching filename per
 call. Returns an empty string when no more files match.
 Each unique filespec maintains its own search context.

2 Parameters
3 filespec
 The file specification, optionally containing wildcards.

2 Examples

 $ file = F$SEARCH("*.TXT")
 $ LOOP:
 $   f = F$SEARCH("[...]*.COM")
 $   IF f .EQS. "" THEN GOTO DONE
 $   WRITE SYS$OUTPUT f
 $   GOTO LOOP
 $ DONE:

1 F$STRING
 Converts an integer expression to a string.

 Format: F$STRING(expression)

 Evaluates the expression and returns its string
 representation.

2 Parameters
3 expression
 The integer expression to convert.

2 Examples

 $ s = F$STRING(42)        ! Returns "42"
 $ t = F$STRING(2 + 3)     ! Returns "5"

1 F$TIME
 Returns the current date and time in VMS format.

 Format: F$TIME()

 Returns a string in the format DD-MMM-YYYY HH:MM:SS.CC.

2 Examples

 $ now = F$TIME()
 $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Current time: ''F$TIME()'"

1 F$TRNLNM
 Translates a logical name.

 Format: F$TRNLNM(logical_name [, table_name])

 Returns the equivalence string for the specified logical
 name. If the logical name is not defined, returns an
 empty string.

2 Parameters
3 logical_name
 The logical name to translate.

3 table_name
 The logical name table to search. Default is
 LNM$FILE_DEV which searches process, job, group,
 and system tables.

2 Examples

 $ sys = F$TRNLNM("SYS$LOGIN")
 $ val = F$TRNLNM("MYLOG", "LNM$SYSTEM_TABLE")
 $ IF F$TRNLNM("WORKDIR") .NES. "" THEN SET DEFAULT WORKDIR

1 F$TYPE
 Returns the data type of a symbol.

 Format: F$TYPE(symbol_name)

 Returns "INTEGER" if the symbol has an integer value,
 "STRING" if it has a string value, or empty string if
 the symbol is not defined.

2 Parameters
3 symbol_name
 The name of the symbol to check.

2 Examples

 $ t = F$TYPE(MY_VAR)
 $ IF F$TYPE(X) .EQS. "" THEN X = 0

1 F$UNIQUE
 Returns a unique number for use in temporary file names.

 Format: F$UNIQUE()

 Returns a unique hexadecimal string suitable for
 constructing unique temporary file names. Each call
 returns a different value.

2 Examples

 $ temp = "TEMP_" + F$UNIQUE() + ".TMP"

1 F$USER
 Returns the username of the current process.

 Format: F$USER()

 Returns the username in uppercase.

2 Examples

 $ user = F$USER()
 $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Logged in as ''F$USER()'"

1 F$VERIFY
 Returns or sets the command verification setting.

 Format: F$VERIFY([new_setting])

 Without arguments, returns the current verification setting
 (0 for off, 1 for on). With an argument, sets the verification
 mode and returns the previous setting.

2 Parameters
3 new_setting
 0 to disable verification, 1 to enable it.

2 Examples

 $ old = F$VERIFY(0)       ! Save and disable verify
 $ ! ... commands ...
 $ old = F$VERIFY(old)     ! Restore previous setting

1 HINTS
 Tips for using OVMX effectively.

 OVMX provides an OpenVMS-compatible environment on Linux. Most
 DCL commands work as they would on a traditional VMS system.

 Key differences from traditional OpenVMS:
   - File versioning uses the native Linux filesystem
   - Device names are mapped from Linux mount points
   - Processes are Linux processes with VMS-style wrappers
   - Privileges are mapped from Linux capabilities

 Getting started:
   - Use SHOW DEFAULT to see your current directory
   - Use DIRECTORY to list files
   - Use SET DEFAULT to change directories
   - Use HELP command-name for detailed help on any command

 Command abbreviation:
   Most commands can be abbreviated to their minimum unique
   prefix. For example, DIR for DIRECTORY, SHO for SHOW.

 Command procedures:
   Files with .COM extension can be executed with @filename.
   Use SET VERIFY to debug command procedures.

 Logical names:
   Logical names provide device-independent file access.
   Use DEFINE or ASSIGN to create logical names.
   Use SHOW LOGICAL to display definitions.

2 Symbols
 DCL symbols store string or integer values.

   $ name = "value"         ! String assignment
   $ count = 42             ! Integer assignment
   $ SHOW SYMBOL name       ! Display a symbol
   $ DELETE/SYMBOL name     ! Remove a symbol

 Global symbols persist across command procedure levels.
 Local symbols are visible only at the current level.

   $ name == "global"       ! Global assignment (==)
   $ name := "also global"  ! Alternative global form

2 FileVersions
 OVMX supports VMS-style file versioning where each edit
 creates a new version (FILE.TXT;1, FILE.TXT;2, etc.).

 Use PURGE to remove old versions.
 Use DELETE FILE.TXT;n to remove a specific version.
 Use ;0 or no version to reference the latest version.

2 Wildcards
 VMS wildcards:
   *   - Matches any characters in a field
   %   - Matches exactly one character
   ... - Matches any directory tree (recursive)

 Examples:
   *.TXT       - All .TXT files
   FILE%.*     - FILE1.DAT, FILEA.TXT, etc.
   [...]*.COM  - All .COM files in any subdirectory

1 CONVENTIONS
 Command line conventions used in OVMX.

 Commands follow this general format:
   $ COMMAND[/QUALIFIER[=value]] [parameter] [parameter]

 Conventions used in help text:
   UPPERCASE   - Keywords (type as shown, any case)
   lowercase   - User-supplied values
   [brackets]  - Optional items
   {braces}    - Choose one of the enclosed items
   ...         - Preceding item can be repeated
   |           - Separates choices

 Special characters:
   @           - Execute a command procedure
   !           - Comment delimiter (rest of line ignored)
   -           - Line continuation (at end of line)
   $           - DCL prompt / command prefix in procedures

 String delimiters:
   "string"    - Double quotes for string literals
   'symbol'    - Single quotes for symbol substitution
   ''symbol''  - Double substitution

2 Abbreviations
 Commands and qualifiers can be abbreviated to their
 minimum unique prefix. For example:
   DIR    for DIRECTORY
   SHO    for SHOW
   DEL    for DELETE
   /CON   for /CONFIRM

2 Expressions
 DCL supports string and integer expressions:

 String operators:
   +   - Concatenation
   -   - Remove trailing spaces then concatenate

 Comparison operators:
   .EQS.  - Equal (string)
   .NES.  - Not equal (string)
   .GTS.  - Greater than (string)
   .GES.  - Greater or equal (string)
   .LTS.  - Less than (string)
   .LES.  - Less or equal (string)

 Numeric operators:
   +, -, *, /   - Arithmetic
   .EQ.  - Equal (numeric)
   .NE.  - Not equal (numeric)
   .GT.  - Greater than (numeric)
   .GE.  - Greater or equal (numeric)
   .LT.  - Less than (numeric)
   .LE.  - Less or equal (numeric)

 Logical operators:
   .AND.  - Logical AND
   .OR.   - Logical OR
   .NOT.  - Logical NOT

1 SPECIFY
 Rules for specifying file names in OVMX.

 A full VMS file specification has this format:
   node::device:[directory]name.type;version

 Components:
   node::       - Network node (not used in OVMX)
   device:      - Device name (e.g., SYS$SYSDEVICE:)
   [directory]  - Directory path
   name         - File name (up to 39 characters)
   .type        - File type/extension
   ;version     - Version number

 Directory specifications:
   [dir]        - Absolute directory
   [.subdir]    - Subdirectory of current default
   [-]          - Parent directory
   [dir.sub]    - Nested directory

 Logical names in filespecs:
   LOGICAL:filename   - Translates the logical name
   SYS$LOGIN:FILE.TXT - File in login directory

2 Defaults
 When components are omitted from a filespec, defaults
 are applied:
   - Device defaults to the current default device
   - Directory defaults to the current default directory
   - Version defaults to the highest existing version
     (for reading) or next version (for creating)

2 Versions
 File versions are positive integers appended after a
 semicolon:
   FILE.TXT;1   - Version 1
   FILE.TXT;2   - Version 2
   FILE.TXT;0   - Latest version (equivalent to omitting)
   FILE.TXT;-1  - Previous version

1 PARAMETERS
 Global parameters available in command procedures.

 Command procedures receive parameters P1 through P8:
   $ @PROC.COM arg1 arg2 arg3

 Inside PROC.COM:
   P1 = "arg1"
   P2 = "arg2"
   P3 = "arg3"
   P4 through P8 = ""

 Parameters are positional. To pass a parameter containing
 spaces, enclose it in quotes:
   $ @PROC.COM "first param" second

 P1 = "first param"
 P2 = "second"

2 Symbols
 In addition to P1-P8, command procedures can access
 these automatic symbols:

   $STATUS   - Status of the last command
   $SEVERITY - Severity of the last command (0-4)
   $RESTART  - TRUE if procedure restarted after error

 Severity levels:
   0 - Warning
   1 - Success
   2 - Error
   3 - Information
   4 - Fatal

1 LEXICAL_FUNCTIONS
 Lexical functions are built-in functions that provide
 string manipulation, system information, and file
 operations within DCL expressions.

 All lexical functions begin with F$ and are called
 within expressions:
   $ result = F$function(arguments)

 Available lexical functions:

 String functions:
   F$EDIT       - Edit a string (trim, compress, etc.)
   F$ELEMENT    - Extract delimited element
   F$EXTRACT    - Extract substring
   F$FAO        - Formatted ASCII output
   F$LENGTH     - String length
   F$LOCATE     - Find substring position

 Conversion functions:
   F$CVSI       - Extract signed bit field
   F$CVTIME     - Convert/extract date/time
   F$CVUI       - Extract unsigned bit field
   F$INTEGER    - Convert to integer
   F$STRING     - Convert to string

 System information:
   F$ENVIRONMENT - DCL environment info
   F$GETDVI     - Device information
   F$GETJPI     - Process information
   F$GETSYI     - System information
   F$MESSAGE    - Status code message text
   F$MODE       - Process mode
   F$PRIVILEGE  - Check privileges
   F$PROCESS    - Process name
   F$TIME       - Current date/time
   F$USER       - Username

 File functions:
   F$DIRECTORY  - Current directory
   F$FILE_ATTRIBUTES - File attributes
   F$PARSE      - Parse file specification
   F$SEARCH     - Wildcard file search

 Symbol functions:
   F$TYPE       - Symbol data type
   F$VERIFY     - Get/set verify mode

 Logical name functions:
   F$TRNLNM     - Translate logical name

 Other:
   F$CONTEXT    - Set process selection context
   F$DEVICE     - Iterate devices
   F$GETQUI     - Queue information
   F$IDENTIFIER - Identifier translation
   F$PID        - Iterate process IDs
   F$UNIQUE     - Generate unique string

 Type HELP F$function-name for detailed help on each function.

1 ON
 Establishes error handling in a command procedure.

 Format: ON severity THEN action

 Sets up an error handler that executes the specified action
 when a command returns an error at or above the specified
 severity level.

2 Parameters
3 severity
 The severity level that triggers the handler:
   WARNING - Severity 0 or above
   ERROR   - Severity 2 or above
   SEVERE_ERROR - Severity 4

3 action
 The action to take:
   CONTINUE - Continue with the next command
   EXIT     - Exit the current procedure level
   GOTO label - Branch to the specified label

2 Examples

 $ ON ERROR THEN GOTO CLEANUP
 $ ON WARNING THEN CONTINUE
 $ ON SEVERE_ERROR THEN EXIT

1 IF
 Conditionally executes a command or block of commands.

 Format: IF expression THEN command
         IF expression THEN
           commands...
         ENDIF

 Evaluates the expression and executes the command(s)
 only if the result is true.

2 Parameters
3 expression
 A logical expression using comparison operators
 (.EQS., .NES., .EQ., .NE., .GT., .LT., etc.)

2 THEN
 Keyword separating the condition from the command(s).
 When THEN appears at the end of a line, begins a
 block IF (terminated by ENDIF).

2 ELSE
 Specifies commands to execute when the IF condition
 is false. Used in block IF constructs.

2 ENDIF
 Terminates a block IF construct.

2 Examples

 $ IF P1 .EQS. "" THEN EXIT
 $ IF count .GT. 10 THEN GOTO DONE
 $ IF F$MODE() .EQS. "INTERACTIVE" THEN -
     WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Interactive session"

 Block IF example:
 $ IF flag
 $ THEN
 $   WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Flag is set"
 $   count = count + 1
 $ ELSE
 $   WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Flag is not set"
 $ ENDIF

1 GOTO
 Transfers control to a labeled line in a command procedure.

 Format: GOTO label

 Branches to the line following the specified label.
 Labels are defined by a line beginning with $ followed
 by the label name and a colon.

2 Parameters
3 label
 The label to branch to.

2 Examples

 $ GOTO START
 $ ...
 $ START:
 $   WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Starting"

1 GOSUB
 Calls a subroutine within a command procedure.

 Format: GOSUB label

 Transfers control to the specified label. Use RETURN
 to return to the line following the GOSUB.

2 Parameters
3 label
 The label of the subroutine.

2 Examples

 $ GOSUB INIT
 $ GOSUB PROCESS_DATA
 $ EXIT
 $
 $ INIT:
 $   count = 0
 $   RETURN
 $
 $ PROCESS_DATA:
 $   ! ... processing ...
 $   RETURN

1 RETURN
 Returns from a GOSUB subroutine call.

 Format: RETURN [status]

 Returns control to the line following the most recent
 GOSUB call.

2 Parameters
3 status
 Optional status code to return.

2 Examples

 $ RETURN
 $ RETURN %X10000001
